"GitHub - Blaze-3/BLAKE3-gpu: Extracting BLAKE3's parallelism using a GPU. · GitHub (https://github.com/Blaze-3/BLAKE3-gpu)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # BLAKE3-gpuParallelizing the BLAKE3 crypto hash function via its merkle tree structure.\nblake3.pdf  | blake3.pdf  |    |  \nfinal-presentation.pdf  | final-presentation.pdf  |    |  \n[Button: View all files]\n\n# BLAKE3-gpu\n\nParallelizing the BLAKE3 crypto hash function via its merkle tree structure.\nCheck Presentation for a complete explanation.\n\n### Current best speedup ⚡ -> 4.5x at 1.07 GiB/s on an octacore\n\n## What\n\nBLAKE3 is a gg crypto hash function. It has good scope for parallelism.\nWe try to extract as much of that parallelism as possible by using GPUs.\nWe also try to speed it up on the CPU with Open-MP and AVX2.\nAll of this is possible due to our new algorithm - Blaze3.\n\n## How\n\n  * [Input] Rewrite the basic, reference implemenation in C++\n  * [Input] Rewrite it again, in CUDA C++\n  * [Input] Make sure all the tests pass (Continuous process)\n  * [Input] Optimize it, fix memory bandwidth issues if they exist (Continuous process)\n\n## Development\n\n  * The `basic` directory has the reference implementations.\n  * A full copy of the original reference implementation is in `testing`.\n  * The blake3 paper is also here for reference.\n  * Openmp work in `openmp`. This version is maxed out for efficency.\n  * Cuda work in `cuda`. This version uses dynamic parallelism.\n  * Dark cuda work happens in `dark`.\n\n## About\n\nExtracting BLAKE3's parallelism using a GPU.\n\n### Resources\n\nReadme\n\n### License\n\nMIT license\n\n### Stars\n\n28 stars\n\n### Watchers\n\n0 watching\n\n### Forks\n\n2 forks--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCryptology ePrint Archive [Mirror] (https://eprint.iacr.asia/2026?offset=100)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Sergio Demian Lerner ... We give the recursive construction, a closed-form formula locating any element in the structured stream, worked examples with illustrations, a security reduction to free-start collision resistance of the hash function's compression step, an optimal element-size analysis for both a flexible toy primitive and real SHA-256, a batched multi-element proof construction, a measured construction-speed comparison against Merkle trees, a concrete application to committing data inside a Bitcoin transaction annex (BIP-341), a design sketch using that application as a data-availability layer for an optimistic Bitcoin rollup, a from-scratch gas-cost comparison, on Ethereum, Rootstock, and Arbitrum, of an in-contract FlatMerkle commitment against an in-contract Merkle tree, tracing the three chains' differing verdicts to specific protocol design choices rather than to anything intrinsic to either construction, and a comparison against the closest prior art we are aware of, authenticated append-only skip lists.\n2026/2468 (PDF)\n\nLast updated:  2026-10-14\n\nFlatMerkle: Logarithmic-Space Inclusion Proofs over a Single Linear Hash Stream\n\nSergio Demian Lerner\n\nShow abstract\n\nCryptographic protocols\n\nMerkle trees are the standard way to obtain logarithmic-size, logarithmic-time inclusion proofs over a large dataset, but they require a tree-shaped hash structure distinct from the ordinary, linear (Merkle--Damg\\aa rd) hashing that most deployed systems -- including Bitcoin's own transaction and witness hashing -- actually perform. We present FlatMerkle, a construction that achieves the same asymptotic guarantees, $O(\\log N)$ proof size and $O(\\log N)$ verification time, over a single ordinary linear hash stream, by periodically re-injecting the hash function's own past internal states (``checkpoints'') back into the stream as ordinary message content. The result is a flat byte sequence whose digest is a single, unmodified invocation of the underlying hash function -- syntactically an ordinary digest of an ordinary byte string, not a distinguishable commitment format -- while still supporting compact inclusion proofs for any element, and compact proofs that a published file is malformed, so that an honest party can always answer a malicious committer in logarithmic space. We give the recursive construction, a closed-form formula locating any element in the structured stream, worked examples with illustrations, a security reduction to free-start collision resistance of the hash function's compression step, an optimal element-size analysis for both a flexible toy primitive and real SHA-256, a batched multi-element proof construction, a measured construction-speed comparison against Merkle trees, a concrete application to committing data inside a Bitcoin transaction annex (BIP-341), a design sketch using that application as a data-availability layer for an optimistic Bitcoin rollup, a from-scratch gas-cost comparison, on Ethereum, Rootstock, and Arbitrum, of an in-contract FlatMerkle commitment against an in-contract Merkle tree, tracing the three chains' differing verdicts to specific protocol design choices rather than to anything intrinsic to either construction, and a comparison against the closest prior art we are aware of, authenticated append-only skip lists.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - akoyapool/akoya-miner · GitHub (https://github.com/akoyapool/akoya-miner)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; The heavy compute runs on the GPU (NVIDIA CUDA or AMD ROCm); the host side handles the pool connection, BLAKE3 keyed-merkle commitments, and share submission. ... On a newer Visual Studio (e.g. 2026) `build.ps1` automatically passes `-allow-unsupported-compiler`; install the VS 2022 build tools if you hit a host-compiler incompatibility.\n# Akoya Miner\n\nThe reference miner for the Akoya Pool.\n\nThis is the official open-source reference implementation of a GPU miner for the Akoya Pool, which mines Pearl (PRL). Use it directly to mine, or as a reference for building your own miner that connects to the pool — the wire protocol it speaks is defined in `proto/v2/miner.proto`.\n\nThe proof of work is a low-rank-noised integer GEMM (matrix-multiply): each candidate is a tile of `A · Bᵀ` that is hashed and checked against a difficulty target. The heavy compute runs on the GPU (NVIDIA CUDA or AMD ROCm); the host side handles the pool connection, BLAKE3 keyed-merkle commitments, and share submission.\n\n## Supported GPUs\n\n`build.sh` auto-detects your card and picks the matching kernel — override with `PEARL_GEMM_ARCH=…` if needed.\n\nArchitecture  | Example GPUs  | Compute (SM)  | `PEARL_GEMM_ARCH`\n--- | --- | --- | ---\nBlackwell  | RTX 50-series (5090 / 5080 …)  | sm_120  | `blackwell`\nBlackwell (DC)  | B200  | sm_100  | `b200`\nHopper  | H100, H200  | sm_90  | `h100`\nAda  | RTX 40-series, L4, L40S, RTX 6000 Ada  | sm_89  | `ada`\nAmpere  | RTX 30-series, A100, A40, A6000  | sm_80 / sm_86  | `ampere`\nTuring  | RTX 20-series, T4  | sm_75  | `turing`\nVolta  | V100  | sm_70  | `volta`\nAMD CDNA3  | MI300X  | gfx942  | `rocm` (`BACKEND=rocm`)\n\nOther NVIDIA cards (sm_70+) build with the `portable` fallback.\n\n## Repository layout\n    \n    `akoya-miner/\n    ├── build.sh                     # one-shot build: native + AOT miner → ./out\n    ├── Akoya.slnx                   # .NET solution\n    ├── proto/v2/miner.proto         # gRPC wire protocol (pool ⇄ miner)\n    ├── src/                         # C# miner (host application)\n    │   ├── Akoya.Miner/             #   entry point, mining loop, pool session\n    │   ├── Akoya.Pool/              #   gRPC connection + session state machine\n    │   ├── Akoya.Crypto/            #   managed BLAKE3 / noise / jackpot\n    │   ├── Akoya.Cuda / .PearlGemm  #   P/Invoke into the native GEMM library\n    │   ├── Akoya.Mining / .MinerCore\n    │   └── Akoya.Proto              #   generated gRPC client stubs\n    └── native/\n        ├── pearl-gemm/              # CUDA + ROCm proof-of-work GEMM kernels\n        │   └── third_party/cutlass  #   NVIDIA CUTLASS (git submodule)\n        ├── pearl-blake3/            # BLAKE3 keyed-merkle (Rust)\n        └── pearl-mining-capi/       # C ABI over pearl-blake3 (Rust → libpearl_mining_capi.so)\n    `\n\nAt runtime the miner loads two native libraries via P/Invoke: `libpearl_gemm_capi.so` (the GPU kernels) and `libpearl_mining_capi.so` (the host-side BLAKE3 merkle). `build.sh` compiles the miner with Native AOT and assembles a self-contained, ready-to-run `./out` folder containing the native binary and both libraries — no .NET runtime required to run it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Nektarios Georgios Tsoutsos (https://dblp.org/pid/134/5020.html)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Chimera: A Hybrid GPU Backend for Sumcheck Acceleration in Zero Knowledge Provers. ... 2026: 1388 (2026)\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/iacr/FarheenT26a\n\nKashfia Farheen, Nektarios Georgios Tsoutsos:\nChimera: A Hybrid GPU Backend for Sumcheck Acceleration in Zero Knowledge Provers. IACR Cryptol. ePrint Arch. 2026: 1388 (2026)\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n[i33]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition @ iacr.org (open access)\n      * details & citations\n \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - itzmeanjan/merklize-blake3: OpenCL powered Merklization using BLAKE3 · GitHub (https://github.com/itzmeanjan/merklize-blake3)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Craw