* The `b3sum` Rust crate, which provides a command line interface. It uses multi-threading by default, making it an order of magnitude faster than e.g. `sha256sum` on typical desktop hardware.\n\n  * The C implementation, which like the Rust implementation includes SIMD code and runtime CPU feature detection on x86. Unlike the Rust implementation, it's not currently multi-threaded. See `c/README.md`.\n\n  * The reference implementation, which is discussed in Section 5.1 of the BLAKE3 paper. This implementation is much smaller and simpler than the optimized ones above. If you want to see how BLAKE3 works, or you're writing a port that doesn't need multi-threading or SIMD optimizations, start here.\n\n  * BLAKE3 was designed by:\n\n  * @oconnor663 (Jack O'Connor)\n  * @sneves (Samuel Neves)\n  * @veorq (Jean-Philippe Aumasson)\n  * @zookozcash (Zooko)\n\nThe development of BLAKE3 was sponsored by Teserakt and Electric Coin Company.\n\nNOTE: BLAKE3 is not a password hashing algorithm, because it's designed to be fast, whereas password hashing should not be fast. If you hash passwords to store the hashes or if you derive keys from passwords, we recommend Argon2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nturboblake 1.8.7 - Docs.rs (https://docs.rs/crate/turboblake/1.8.7)\nciteturn3search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   *     * 1.8.7 (2026-10-26) ... BLAKE3 is a Merkle tree, and Bend2 runs tree-shaped work in parallel on every CPU core and the GPU. ... Benchmarks and method: gunta.github.io/BLAKE3-bend.\n\n  * Versions\n  *     * 1.8.7 (2026-10-26)\n\n  * Owners\n  * turboblake-1.8.7 is not a library.\n\n# turboblake\n\nBLAKE3, turbocharged: the official `b3sum` (1.8.7) on `blake3-bend`, a drop-in for `b3sum` and `sha256sum`. Every flag, every output format and every error message is upstream's (upstream's own CLI tests pass), and in the default mode many files and big ones are hashed at once by a Bend2 engine: opened in parallel, mapped or read, files not in the page cache streamed at SSD speed, every file's tree on every core. BLAKE3 is a Merkle tree, and Bend2 runs tree-shaped work in parallel on every CPU core and the GPU.\n    \n    `brew install gunta/tap/turboblake\n    `\n    \n    `cargo install turboblake\n    `\n\nHomebrew installs a prebuilt binary (macOS and Linux, arm64 and x86-64); `cargo install` builds from source, tuned for your CPU, and needs clang (Apple's, or LLVM 19+). `cargo binstall turboblake` fetches the prebuilt binaries through cargo.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nfeat(blake3): the BLAKE3 compression function over uint32 lanes · Issue #14 · fractalyze/hash-frx · GitHub (https://github.com/fractalyze/hash-frx/issues/14)\nciteturn3search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last week; opened on Jul 28, 2026 ... is parallel by construction, which is exactly the shape a GPU wants for Merkle ... Rejected in the design discussion: BLAKE3's tree mode is what makes it parallel,\n\n  1. fractalyze\n  2. hash-frx\n\n## Repository navigation\n\n  *   * Code\n  * Issues30 (30)\n  * Pull requests14 (14)\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality\n  * Insights\n\nMore items\n\n# feat(blake3): the BLAKE3 compression function over uint32 lanes #14\n\nClosed\n\nFeature\n\nClosed\n\nfeat(blake3): the BLAKE3 compression function over uint32 lanes#14\n\nFeature\n\nCopy link\n\nAssignees\n\nLabels\n\narea:blake3BLAKE3 compression, chunk chaining, tree mode, XOFBLAKE3 compression, chunk chaining, tree mode, XOF\n\nMilestone\n\nBLAKE3 and Keccak\n\n## Description\n\nchokobole\n\nopened on Jul 28, 2026\n\nIssue body actions\n\n### Pre-submission checklist\n\n  * [Input] I have searched the existing issues and this is not a duplicate.\n\n### Context / problem\n\nBLAKE3 is the byte hash this stack is missing at the fast end: tree-mode, so it\nis parallel by construction, which is exactly the shape a GPU wa